{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1 and use it to sign the host key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A protected ED25519 CA was created and signed node1's ED25519 host key. The resulting host certificate identifies the expected CA and contains principals for node1 and 10.100.58.10.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bd023b19865244d9"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Configure node1's SSH server to present the CA-signed host certificate.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The SSH configuration validated, reloaded successfully, and reported the host certificate directive. After restart, sshd remained active and presented the same effective certificate configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5a154735c1c7425f","cmd-99847efd6c1f4818"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Demonstrate that a client trusting only the authority can connect without pinning node1's host key.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A strict SSH connection using a known-hosts file containing only an @cert-authority entry succeeded. Client diagnostics directly show that the presented host certificate matched the trusted CA, including again after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e03c8e4cd08049bd","cmd-99847efd6c1f4818"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"No causally unrelated or reverted troubleshooting mutations were identified. The CA, certificate, and sshd configuration changes were required task work; the test credential and reboot were used for bounded verification and the mandatory restart protocol. Hygiene is reduced because verification artifacts remained: cmd-29039957635f44cf left a test private key, its root authorized_keys entry, and a test trust file under /root/.ssh, while cmd-e03c8e4cd08049bd and cmd-99847efd6c1f4818 left two debug logs in /tmp. These were not cleaned after verification. Otherwise, the final snapshot shows no failed services, unchanged listening-service exposure, and healthy package state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-29039957635f44cf","cmd-e03c8e4cd08049bd","cmd-99847efd6c1f4818","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct configuration and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete: node1 was healthy before restart, acknowledged the scheduled reboot, became temporarily unreachable, returned with a different boot identity, and recovered sshd, its listener, certificate configuration, and CA-trusted SSH behavior. Fixed lifecycle observations independently confirm the boot-identity change.","limitations":["The client test originated on node1 and connected through node1's managed IP; it validates the real sshd certificate exchange and CA-only trust decision but does not exercise an external client's network path.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /root/.ssh, so retained test credentials are established from the chronological command trace and absence of any later cleanup command rather than a final filesystem inventory."]}
